COMMAND BEHAVIOUR\
\
validate:\
Check data, labels, references, prompt presence and schemas.\
Report confirmed versus proposed label counts separately.\
Unconfirmed labels are an expected readiness gap, not malformed data.\
\
show-case:\
Display the case, context, fixed response and rubric.\
Clearly separate proposed labels from confirmed reviews.\
\
judge:\
Evaluate the fixed responses.\
Create a new evidence run.\
Keep machine assessments separate from human labels.\
\
calibrate:\
Compare fixed-response judge assessments with matching, confirmed,\
current human reviews.\
Report development and holdout results separately.\
\
latest-fixed:\
Resolve the most recent finalised fixed-response judge run.\
Print its ID and mode.\
Never substitute an older successful run to hide newer errors.\
Reject unfinished, corrupted or ambiguous run metadata.\
A finalised run with individual evaluation errors may still be\
calibrated, but missing/invalid evaluations must be explicit.\
\
If no human labels are confirmed:\
- Produce a useful report.\
- State that confirmed human agreement is unavailable.\
- Show confirmed-label coverage as 0/8.\
- Do not report agreement as 0% or 100%.\
- Optionally show proposed-label comparison clearly marked provisional.\
\
Report:\
- Agreement count and valid paired denominator.\
- Missing/unconfirmed/stale human labels.\
- Evaluation errors.\
- False passes and false fails relative to confirmed human labels.\
- Per-dimension disagreements.\
- Critical disagreements.\
\
Replay calibration must say it is a workflow demonstration, not\
validation of a live judge.\
\
compare:\
Generate and evaluate an answer for both prompts for each selected\
case ID. Preserve repeated input cases as separately identified\
observations and disclose their dependence.\
\
Keep the response model and supported generation settings the same\
for both prompts.\
Use a frozen judge prompt, rubric and judge configuration.\
Randomise A/B execution order with a recorded local seed.\
Do not claim the local seed makes model responses reproducible.\
Hide A/B labels from the judge.\
\
For all eight case IDs, a successful run normally has:\
- 16 response-generation calls.\
- 16 judge calls.\
Track actual attempts, failures and retries separately.\
\
Do not reuse fixed-response human labels for generated answers.\
Require human review before presenting a final prompt selection.\
\
Present:\
- Per-case responses, scores and reasons.\
- Critical violations.\
- Pass/fail/error counts.\
- Measured usage and timing where available.\
- Human-review status.\
- A provisional preference or "inconclusive".\
\
Never force a winner.\
If both prompts show critical failures, do not recommend either\
for approval on this evidence.\
\
repeat:\
Generate a fresh response on each repetition and judge that response.\
For three cases repeated three times, normally:\
- Nine generation calls.\
- Nine judge calls.\
Track actual calls and errors separately.\
\
Report:\
- All responses and assessments.\
- Pass count out of three attempted repeats per case.\
- Valid-evaluation count and errors.\
- Score ranges over valid evaluations.\
- Critical violations.\
- Prompt and model configuration.\
\
Explain that variation may come from response generation, judging\
or both. This experiment does not isolate those causes.\
Three successful observations do not guarantee future reliability.\
\
summary:\
Show which activities actually ran.\
Distinguish completed, failed, partially completed and not run.\
Keep live and replay evidence separate.\
Display run IDs and version differences.\
Do not combine unrelated runs into one apparent experiment.\
Do not mark human review complete unless confirmed records exist.\
